### Rotating credentials

A credential that rotates belongs in the **provider** block's `headers`, and nowhere else.

Do not put it in a resource's own `headers`: that attribute forces replacement as a *whole map*,
so rotating one key proposes a destroy-and-create of every resource naming it.

Do not repeat it in `delete_headers` either. Terraform sends no configuration to a destroy, so the
provider captures those into private state when the resource is created or updated and replays the
capture on destroy -- where it overrides the provider's value. Rotate the credential and the destroy
goes out with the old one; if the endpoint rejects it, that destroy fails, and when the destroy is
the first half of the replacement `headers` just forced, the replacement never completes. State then
keeps the old value, so the next plan proposes the same replacement, and no later configuration edit
reaches it -- the state write that would fix it is the one the failure prevents.

Provider configuration is re-evaluated on every operation and is never written to state, so a
credential kept only there is invisible to the plan and always current. It reaches create, read,
refresh, destroy and the read an import performs. The provider warns when `delete_headers` names a
header it also sets.

